#N canvas 0 0 925 1131 10;
#X declare -lib else;
#X text 24 20 dddd - structured data transport for Pd-Lua developers;
#N canvas 0 0 925 1131 api 0;
#X text 24 20 dddd Lua API;
#X text 24 55 Load the module with: local dddd = require("dddd"), f 112;
#X text 24 100 CONSTRUCTION AND RECEPTION;
#X text 24 130 dddd:new(pdobj \, atoms) - parse Pd atoms as the contents of an implicit outer list and return a dddd instance., f 112;
#X text 24 165 dddd:new_from_table(pdobj \, table) - wrap an existing Lua table. Construction does not copy the table., f 112;
#X text 24 200 dddd:new_from_atoms(pdobj \, atoms) - resolve atoms[1] and deep-copy the temporary registered payload. This is the normal receiver constructor., f 112;
#X text 24 250 dddd:new_from_id(pdobj \, id) - resolve one temporary ID directly. Prefer new_from_atoms inside in_X_dddd., f 112;
#X text 24 285 value:get_dddd_from_id(pdobj \, id) - legacy alternate lookup. The current implementation is defective. Prefer new_from_id., f 112;
#X text 24 335 TRANSPORT AND ACCESS;
#X text 24 365 value:output(outlet) - synchronously emit dddd <id> from the one-based Pd outlet., f 112;
#X text 24 400 value:get_table() - return the complete local Lua payload by reference. Local edits change this dddd instance., f 112;
#X text 24 435 value:get_depth(any_value) - return sequence depth. Scalars are depth 0 and tables are at least depth 1, f 112;
#X text 24 470 value:get_table_depth() - calculate the current payload's sequence depth., f 112;
#X text 24 520 PARSING \, INSPECTION \, AND LOCAL TYPES;
#X text 24 550 value:lisplist_to_table(string) - parse one complete (...) or [...] expression., f 112;
#X text 24 585 value:table_from_atoms(atoms) - parse Pd atoms with an implicit outer list. With no delimiters it defaults to square brackets., f 112;
#X text 24 620 value:to_string(value) / value:print() - serialize or print sequence keys only. Associative fields are omitted., f 112;
#X text 24 655 value:set_type(name) / get_type() / assert_type(name) - local wrapper metadata. Type tags are not transmitted., f 112;
#X restore 27 180 pd api;
#N canvas 0 0 1860 1131 architecture 0;
#X text 24 20 Architecture and message lifetime;
#X text 24 60 WHY INDIRECTION EXISTS;
#X text 24 90 Pd patch cords carry flat atoms. dddd keeps the recursive value in Lua and sends a short token through Pd instead of flattening the payload., f 112;
#X text 24 145 ONE SYNCHRONOUS TRANSMISSION;
#X text 24 175 1 output() generates a 12-character hexadecimal ID and stores the value in _G.dddd_outlets[id]., f 112;
#X text 24 210 2 The sender emits selector dddd with <id> as its only atom., f 112;
#X text 24 245 3 Pd dispatches the message to each connected in_X_dddd handler., f 112;
#X text 24 280 4 Each receiver resolves the ID and recursively copies the payload., f 112;
#X text 24 315 5 When synchronous dispatch returns \, output() removes the registry entry., f 112;
#X text 24 365 VALUE SEMANTICS AT FAN-OUT;
#X text 24 395 Every receiver starts from an independent copy. Destructive transformations on one branch do not affect sibling branches \, so results do not depend on connection order., f 112;
#X text 24 455 REGISTRY SCOPE AND FAILURE MODEL;
#X text 24 485 Sender and receiver must share the same Lua global environment. Separate registries cannot resolve each other's IDs., f 112;
#X text 24 520 IDs expire when the synchronous outlet call returns. They are transport tokens \, not persistent references or security identifiers., f 112;
#X text 24 570 PERFORMANCE;
#X text 24 600 Work grows approximately with payload size times receiver count. Profile large structures \, dense message rates \, and broad fan-out., f 112;
#X text 24 650 dddd is appropriate for control-rate structures. Do not use it for signal vectors or sample-by-sample audio., f 112;
#X restore 27 207 pd architecture;
#N canvas 0 0 925 1131 data-model 0;
#X text 24 20 Data model and nested-list syntax;
#X text 24 60 PAYLOADS;
#X text 24 90 A dddd normally wraps a recursive Lua table. Sequence keys represent ordered material. Associative keys represent semantic fields such as pitches \, duration \, color \, or children., f 112;
#X text 24 150 TEXT FORMAT;
#X text 24 180 (1 2 (3 4) five) and [1 2 [3 4] five] both represent {1 \, 2 \, {3 \, 4} \, "five"}., f 112;
#X text 24 215 Whitespace separates tokens. tonumber(token) creates numbers and every other token becomes a string., f 112;
#X text 24 250 Use only one delimiter style per expression. Empty lists work. Quoting and escaping are not supported., f 112;
#X text 24 300 COMPLETE EXPRESSIONS AND PD ATOMS;
#X text 24 330 lisplist_to_table parses a complete expression including its outer delimiters., f 112;
#X text 24 365 table_from_atoms treats the atoms as list contents and supplies an implicit outer list. Existing outer delimiters therefore create another nested level., f 112;
#X text 24 415 SEQUENCE AND ASSOCIATIVE TABLES;
#X text 24 445 Transport and get_table preserve all keys. to_string \, print \, and get_depth traverse consecutive integer keys only., f 112;
#X text 24 495 DEPTH;
#X text 24 525 42 has depth 0 {1 \, 2} has depth 1 {1 \, {2 \, 3}} has depth 2, f 112;
#X text 24 575 CURRENT BOUNDARIES;
#X text 24 605 Payloads must be acyclic. Text syntax covers numbers \, unquoted strings \, and nested sequences. Prefer direct Lua tables for record-like data., f 112;
#X text 24 655 Type tags are local metadata. Put a namespaced type/version field inside the payload when it must cross a connection., f 112;
#X restore 27 234 pd data-model;
#N canvas 0 0 925 1131 getting-started 1;
#X text 24 20 Getting started;
